{ This study is designed to reinforce Levy Rozman's instruction on the Caro-Kann Defense in this video: https://youtu.be/rmbU97iftC8

I recommend watching the video and then use this study to practice your Caro-Kann! }
1. e4 { If you know the Caro-Kann, go ahead and play it. } 1... c6 2. d4 { Given the opportunity White likes to put two pawns in the center. What next? } 2... d5 { Rozman explains this opening as having three straightforward branches from this point based on what white does with this pawn.
- Option 1 - White Captures
- Option 2 - White Pushes
- Option 3 - White Defends the Pawn
The secret fourth option is that they forget that it is unguarded, in which case you take it. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4e5,Ge4d5] } *


[Event "🦇Gotham's Caro-Kann Practice: Option 1 - White Captures #1"]
[Date "2021.02.08"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B13"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[StudyName "🦇Gotham's Caro-Kann Practice"]
[ChapterName "Option 1 - White Captures #1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Ov8HP8iB/5pCbPO1K"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/fletchgraham"]
[UTCDate "2021.02.08"]
[UTCTime "14:10:56"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Here we'll look at White's first option, to take your pawn. So go ahead and play the Caro-Kann up to that point. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 { This is that first option - White decides to capture. How do you respond? } 3... cxd5 4. Nf3 { White chooses to develop normally. This only happens in 1/50 database games, but I suspect Rozman reviews this line because it is common at beginner and intermediate levels.
Your next moves are automatic developing steps, knights before bishops yada yada.. } 4... Nc6 (4... Nf6 { People do this but one problem is it allows Harvey Dent to swoop into e5 early. } { [%cal Gf3e5] }) 5. Be2 Nf6 6. Nc3 { What bishop move did Gotham show in the video? } 6... Bf5 { Yep! If you had gotten pinned perhaps you would play Bd7. Bg4 is good if you can pin the knight, but right now White's bishop is preventing that. } { [%csl Gb5,Gg4][%cal Gc8d7] } (6... Bg4 { Another valid move mentioned by Rozman but not he one he demonstrated. }) 7. O-O { Now that our light-square bishop is out, we're permitted to play... } 7... e6 8. Bf4 Be7 { This bishop could also come to d6. } { [%cal Ge7d6] } 9. Re1 O-O { Cool, Levy got us to a position that the engine shows is slightly better for Black.
He says then now you might want to play on the queenside or sometimes hop your night up to the middle of the board. } { [%cal Ga7a6,Gb7b5,Ga8c8,Gf6e4] } *


[Event "🦇Gotham's Caro-Kann Practice: Option 1 - White Captures #2"]
